uying":false,"_score_insider_buying":0.0,"pf":["flagged","paper"],"description_full":"Comfort Systems USA, Inc., together with its subsidiaries, provides mechanical and electrical installation, renovation, maintenance, repair, and replacement services for the mechanical and electrical services industry in the United States. The company operates through two segments: Mechanical and Electrical. It offers heating, ventilation, and air conditioning systems, as well as plumbing, electrical, piping and controls, off-site construction, monitoring, and fire protection. The company is also involved in the design, engineering, integration, installation, and start-up of mechanical, electrical, and plumbing (MEP) and related systems in new buildings; and renovation, expansion, maintenance, monitoring, repair, and replacement of systems in existing buildings. In addition, it provides remote monitoring of power usage, temperature, pressure, humidity and air flow for MEP and other building systems. The company serves building owners and developers, general contractors, architects, consulting engineers, and property managers in the commercial, industrial, and institutional markets. Comfort Systems USA, Inc. was founded in 1917 and is headquartered in Houston, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Brian E. Lane | CEO & Director | Age 68 | Compensation: $4.4M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 22K people. Revenue per employee of $401K ranks in the above the sector median among Industrials peers, with revenue growing 15%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $45K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $961 per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 11\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (5/10).","financial_summary":["Comfort Systems USA, Inc. is a large-cap industrials business with a $39.5B market cap on $9.1B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +30% over the past three years and +19% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 30% against a 11% cost of capital \u2014 a 19-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat, and 19% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $1.0B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 11 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.16\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.7%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 63% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.6292752404351686},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Heavy insider selling (net $103.0M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-102957925.72},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Supply chain reshoring costs and labour shortages \u2014 onshoring mandates raise capex requirements while a shrinking skilled-labour pool inflates operating costs.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Reinvestment rate supports 17% organic growth capacity \u2014 capital is compounding inside the business","Piotroski score 7/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency","Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","20 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","ROIC sits in the top quartile of Industrials peers \u2014 the moat is wider than the neighbourhood","Growing faster than most Industrials peers \u2014 taking share inside the profit pool"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Can Comfort Systems USA (NYSE:FIX) Sustain Its Strong Backlog Momentum?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/technical-analysis/can-comfort-systems-usa-nysefix-sustain-its-strong-backlog-momentum","date":"2026-11-26","timestamp":1795720860.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.899,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Comfort Systems USA (FIX) Stock Looks Undervalued With Earnings Support","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/capital-goods/nyse-fix/comfort-systems-usa/news/comfort-systems-usa-fix-stock-looks-undervalued-with-earning","date":"2026-11-24","timestamp":1795536627.023,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.979,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"Comfort Systems USA (FIX) Rebounds Sharply, Is The Stock Still Cheap?","source":"finance.yahoo.com","link":"https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/comfort-systems-usa-fix-rebounds-111647756.html","date":"2026-11-24","timestamp":1795519007.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.961,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Comfort Systems USA (FIX) Stock Gets Fair Value Trim As Analysts Weigh Backlog And Marg